## 1. Objective and scope

Determine the causal reasons why nominally identical 3D-printed polymer tensile coupons show persistent differences in tensile strength.

The study will:

- Quantify variation attributable to:
  - feedstock and material condition;
  - printer, nozzle, build plate, and maintenance state;
  - process settings and actual process behavior;
  - coupon position, orientation, and build timing;
  - geometry and internal defects;
  - storage and conditioning;
  - tensile-test setup and execution.
- Distinguish systematic causal effects from random within-build, between-build, printer, operator, and testing variability.
- Test competing mechanisms, including:
  1. differences in interlayer bonding caused by thermal history or cooling;
  2. under-extrusion, porosity, or dimensional variation;
  3. feedstock moisture or batch effects;
  4. machine-specific calibration or wear;
  5. spatial or temporal gradients within builds;
  6. post-print aging or conditioning;
  7. tensile-machine, gripping, alignment, or operator effects.
- Estimate effects for the selected polymer, geometry, printing process, and operating range; broader generalization will require replication across materials and printer models.

Primary outcome: ultimate tensile strength calculated using independently measured cross-sectional area. Secondary outcomes: failure load, modulus, yield strength, elongation at break, failure location and mode, density/void fraction, and dimensional accuracy.

## 2. Assumptions

- Coupons are produced from traceable feedstock and conform to one selected tensile-coupon standard or a prespecified geometry.
- Tensile strength is a well-defined response only when specimen dimensions, conditioning, strain rate, and failure validity are controlled.
- Potential causes are represented in a directed acyclic graph (DAG) before data collection. A provisional structure is:

  - feedstock lot, moisture, machine condition, settings, ambient conditions, and build position  
    → actual thermal/extrusion history  
    → geometry, porosity, crystallinity, and interlayer bonding  
    → tensile strength;
  - operator, tensile frame, grips, alignment, and analysis choices  
    → measured strength;
  - maintenance status and production time may affect both process measurements and outcomes.

- Randomization makes assigned process factors independent of latent determinants of strength within the defined study.
- Stable-unit assumptions are reasonable: one coupon does not materially affect another except through measurable shared build effects. Shared-build dependence will nevertheless be modeled.
- Measurements do not modify tensile properties, except where destructive characterization is assigned to matched or separate specimens.
- No exclusions, transformations, or model changes will be based on whether they increase statistical significance.
- Missingness, machine stoppages, print failures, and invalid tensile tests are potentially informative and will be recorded as outcomes rather than silently discarded.

## 3. Research design

### Phase A: Measurement-system qualification

Before causal testing:

1. Qualify dimensional instruments, load cells, extensometers, thermocouples, moisture measurements, and any imaging method.
2. Conduct crossed gauge repeatability and reproducibility studies:
   - multiple operators repeatedly measure the same retained reference parts;
   - the same calibration specimens are tested, where nondestructive;
   - tensile frames are checked using traceable standards and standardized alignment procedures.
3. Introduce blinded duplicate measurements and coded specimens.
4. Establish rules for grip slippage, off-gauge failure, premature failure, data loss, and machine alarms.

The main study proceeds only if measurement-system variation is acceptably small relative to expected coupon variation, using prespecified thresholds.

### Phase B: Variance decomposition under nominally identical conditions

Produce coupons at locked nominal settings using multiple:

- feedstock lots/spools;
- independently dried or conditioned feedstock batches;
- printers of the same model, where available;
- builds per printer and lot;
- plate positions;
- printing days and time blocks;
- operators for printing and testing;
- tensile frames or fixtures, where relevant.

Use balanced allocation and randomize:

- specimen identifiers;
- plate position where geometrically possible;
- build order;
- assignment of coupons to conditioning batches;
- test order;
- tensile frame, grip set, and operator.

Within each build, distribute specimens across the plate rather than grouping them by downstream test condition. Test personnel remain blinded to printer, lot, build position, and process history.

This phase estimates how much variation occurs at the lot, printer, build, plate-location, coupon, conditioning-batch, test-frame, and operator levels. It also identifies reproducible clusters without yet interpreting association as causation.

### Phase C: Randomized causal intervention study

Use a blocked factorial or fractional-factorial design selected after Phase B. Candidate manipulable factors include:

- feedstock moisture/conditioning level;
- nozzle temperature;
- bed temperature;
- chamber or local ambient temperature;
- print speed and volumetric flow;
- cooling-fan setting;
- layer height;
- raster orientation;
- extrusion multiplier;
- nozzle condition or controlled nozzle replacement;
- build-plate position and coupon orientation;
- printer calibration state;
- post-print conditioning time, temperature, and humidity;
- tensile crosshead rate, alignment protocol, or grip configuration.

Factors should be varied over safe, practically relevant ranges. Continuous factors should preferably have at least three levels or a response-surface design to detect nonlinearity. Implausible or unsafe combinations are excluded before randomization.

Design features:

- Block by feedstock lot, printer, day, and build where needed.
- Randomize settings within the smallest feasible unit:
  - build-level settings at the build level;
  - specimen-level conditioning and test assignments at the specimen level.
- Include repeated center-point/nominal-setting builds throughout the schedule to detect drift.
- Include replicated treatment combinations across days, printers, and feedstock lots.
- Avoid changing several factors together unless the design preserves estimability of their main effects and key interactions.
- Preselect interactions motivated by mechanisms, such as temperature × cooling, moisture × temperature, speed × extrusion multiplier, and orientation × layer bonding.
- Base sample size on simulation or analytical power calculations using Phase B variance components, the smallest practically important strength difference, clustering, multiplicity, and expected invalid-test rates.

### Phase D: Mechanism-discrimination study

For selected randomized contrasts, collect matched mechanistic measurements using sister coupons or sacrificial coupons from the same randomized build locations.

Key competing explanations and distinguishing interventions include:

- **Thermal-history/interlayer-bonding mechanism:** manipulate nozzle/chamber temperature and cooling while holding feedstock and nominal material deposition constant.
- **Porosity/under-extrusion mechanism:** manipulate calibrated flow or volumetric rate, and measure mass, geometry, density, and void structure.
- **Moisture mechanism:** randomly assign feedstock portions to controlled moisture states, confirm moisture before printing, and hold printer settings constant.
- **Machine-calibration or wear mechanism:** use a randomized crossover in which the same feedstock and settings are run before and after calibration/nozzle replacement on each printer.
- **Build-position mechanism:** randomize specimen locations and rotate or mirror layouts across builds.
- **Test-system mechanism:** randomly assign blinded coupons from the same builds across operators, frames, grip setups, and test order.
- **Aging/conditioning mechanism:** randomly assign coupons to storage duration and humidity conditions after printing.

Where feasible, include reversals or rescue interventions. For example, if a weak printer improves after nozzle replacement while matched control printers do not, this supports a nozzle-related cause more strongly than a persistent printer label effect.

## 4. Data or evidence needed

Record a complete specimen-level provenance chain:

- unique coupon, build, printer, nozzle, plate, spool, and feedstock-lot identifiers;
- polymer formulation, colorant/additive information, manufacturing lot, spool age, and storage history;
- measured feedstock moisture before and after conditioning;
- printer firmware, slicer version, model file hash, toolpath file hash, and all process settings;
- maintenance, calibration, nozzle-use hours, nozzle diameter, and known faults;
- planned and actual build position, orientation, sequence, and surrounding-part layout;
- start/end timestamps and elapsed time since earlier builds;
- ambient temperature and humidity;
- logged nozzle, bed, and chamber temperatures;
- extrusion/flow telemetry, power interruptions, alarms, and print failures;
- coupon mass and dimensional measurements at prespecified locations;
- conditioning temperature, humidity, duration, and time from print completion to test;
- tensile frame, load cell, calibration state, operator, grips, alignment checks, test order, and crosshead/strain rate;
- raw force, displacement, extensometer strain, and timestamps;
- fracture location, angle, surface, layer relation, and grip slippage;
- images of coupons before and after testing;
- nondestructive or destructive defect and microstructure measurements from selected specimens;
- all deviations, exclusions, missing measurements, reprints, and reruns.

Preserve raw machine logs and raw tensile curves. Maintain a version-controlled data dictionary, protocol, DAG, randomization schedule, and analysis plan.

## 5. Measurement

### Outcomes

- **Ultimate tensile strength:** maximum valid force divided by measured pretest cross-sectional area at the failure-relevant gauge location.
- **Failure load:** analyzed separately to distinguish true material/process effects from area-normalization effects.
- **Other mechanical outcomes:** modulus, yield strength, elongation at break, toughness, and uncertainty in each estimate.
- **Failure validity:** gauge failure, grip failure, slippage, delamination, brittle/ductile appearance, and off-axis fracture.

Report both engineering stress based on specified nominal area and stress based on measured area, while designating one as primary in advance.

### Process and material measurements

- Coupon width and thickness at multiple gauge locations using a calibrated method with controlled contact force.
- Mass and apparent density.
- Feedstock diameter sampled along each spool.
- Moisture using a validated method appropriate to the polymer.
- Actual nozzle, bed, chamber, and nearby-air temperatures; do not rely solely on setpoints.
- Thermal history near representative coupons where instrumentation does not materially disturb printing.
- Porosity/void fraction and void orientation using validated imaging on a stratified subset.
- Interlayer geometry, bead width, neck growth, fracture-surface features, and, where appropriate, crystallinity or molecular degradation indicators.
- Alignment and bending strain in tensile testing using a standardized verification method.

### Quality controls

- Calibrate all instruments on a defined schedule.
- Use duplicate measurements on a random subset.
- Blind metrology and tensile operators to treatment and prior results.
- Predefine signal-processing methods, tensile-curve segmentation, and invalidity criteria.
- Quantify measurement error and propagate it into uncertainty estimates where material.
- Use reference coupons or check standards over time to detect instrument drift.

## 6. Analysis

### Causal estimands

Specify before analysis:

- average treatment effects of each randomized manufacturing and testing factor on tensile strength;
- selected treatment interactions;
- effects within printer, lot, or orientation strata when scientifically justified;
- proportion of the observed nominal-condition variance eliminated by each intervention;
- mechanistic indirect effects only as secondary estimands.

Use intention-to-treat estimates based on assigned conditions as primary. Effects of achieved temperatures, moisture, or flow are secondary because achieved values may be post-randomization variables; estimate them using assignment as an instrument only if assumptions are defensible.

### Statistical models

1. **Measurement-system analysis**
   - Estimate repeatability, reproducibility, drift, and operator/instrument components.
   - Determine whether measurement error could explain the observed differences.

2. **Variance decomposition**
   - Fit hierarchical models with random effects for lot, printer, day, build, conditioning batch, frame, test operator, and residual coupon variation.
   - Treat plate coordinates and production time as structured variables rather than arbitrary group labels.
   - Report variance components and prediction intervals with uncertainty.

3. **Randomized-factor analysis**
   - Fit multilevel factorial or response-surface models reflecting the randomization and blocking structure.
   - Include prespecified interactions and nonlinear terms.
   - Use cluster-appropriate standard errors or full hierarchical uncertainty.
   - Report effect sizes and confidence or credible intervals, not significance alone.
   - Adjust confirmatory inferences for multiple primary contrasts using a prespecified procedure.

4. **Spatial and temporal analysis**
   - Test for plate-location gradients, center-edge effects, directional patterns, warm-up effects, and drift.
   - Use randomized layout permutations for confirmatory spatial tests where appropriate.

5. **Mechanism analysis**
   - First establish that the randomized intervention changes strength.
   - Then test whether it changes the proposed mediator in the predicted direction.
   - Examine whether mediator changes precede or accompany changes in strength.
   - Treat simple mediator-adjusted regressions cautiously because mediator–outcome confounding may remain.
   - Prefer separate randomized mediator interventions or factorial designs when claiming mediation.

6. **Testing-artifact analysis**
   - Compare the same randomized production strata across frames, operators, grip configurations, and order.
   - Determine whether differences appear in measured strength without corresponding differences in failure load, geometry, defects, or fracture morphology.

### Predictions that distinguish explanations

- **Interlayer bonding:** strength differences should be largest for orientations loading layer interfaces, respond to thermal/cooling interventions, and correspond to interlayer fracture or neck-growth measures.
- **Porosity/under-extrusion:** weaker specimens should show lower mass/density or greater void fraction; calibrated flow changes should shift both defects and strength.
- **Moisture:** randomized moisture states should alter strength and possibly surface/void signatures; drying should rescue the effect when printing and testing remain fixed.
- **Machine condition:** a machine effect should follow calibration/nozzle state in crossover tests rather than remain tied to the printer identifier.
- **Plate position:** weakness should track randomized physical coordinates and recur after mirrored layouts, not coupon labels or test order.
- **Post-print conditioning:** strength should track randomized storage exposure and time, despite common build origin.
- **Testing artifact:** apparent differences should follow frame/operator/grip assignment, or disappear after alignment correction, without matching material evidence.
- **Dimensional normalization:** differences in calculated strength but not failure load that shrink when local measured area replaces nominal area support a geometry-calculation explanation.

### Robustness and falsification

- Repeat principal effects in held-out builds, days, printers, and feedstock lots.
- Check treatment-balance and randomization integrity.
- Analyze invalid tests and print failures by treatment assignment.
- Compare models with and without prespecified influential observations, without deleting valid data.
- Conduct sensitivity analyses for measurement error, missing data, protocol deviations, and model form.
- Use negative controls where feasible, such as variables recorded after random assignment that should not depend on treatment.
- Do not interpret observational correlations from Phase B as causal unless reproduced through intervention or a justified quasi-experimental contrast.

## 7. Decision / stopping rule

Before collection, define:

- the smallest practically important difference in tensile strength;
- required precision for primary effects;
- acceptable measurement-system variation;
- maximum invalid-test and print-failure rates;
- primary factors, interactions, outcomes, and replication strata.

A factor will be considered a supported causal contributor only if:

1. it was randomized or evaluated through a credible crossover/quasi-experimental intervention;
2. its estimated effect exceeds the practical threshold or rules out effects large enough to matter;
3. the interval estimate has the prespecified precision;
4. the effect replicates across independent builds and is not driven by a single lot, printer, operator, or frame;
5. manipulation and protocol checks support treatment fidelity;
6. predicted auxiliary observations are present and key rival predictions are absent.

A mechanism will be considered provisionally supported when the intervention changes both strength and the proposed mechanistic measurement in the predicted temporal and directional pattern, and when a rescue or targeted intervention reproduces or reverses the outcome. Association alone will not establish mediation.

Candidate explanations will be deprioritized when adequately powered randomized contrasts bound their effects below the practical threshold across the intended operating range.

Stop enrollment when all prespecified treatment cells and independent replication requirements are complete and precision targets are met. Do not stop early for favorable significance unless a formal group-sequential rule was prespecified. Pause the study for calibration failure, excessive protocol violations, treatment-dependent catastrophic failures, loss of environmental control, or invalid-test rates exceeding the threshold.

Conclude that residual variation remains unexplained if the validated measurement error plus modeled known causes leaves a reproducible residual component above the practical threshold after planned interventions. That result should trigger a new, separately preregistered study rather than post hoc factor searching.

## 8. Limitations

- Conclusions apply primarily to the selected polymer formulation, geometry, slicer, printer architecture, and process range.
- Some process variables cannot be randomized at the coupon level, so build-level clustering may limit power.
- Instrumenting the print process may alter thermal or airflow conditions.
- Internal imaging and microstructural assays may be destructive, sparse, or imperfect proxies for bond strength.
- Feedstock lots and nominally identical printers may contain unmeasured compositional or hardware differences.
- Actual process variables are often consequences of assigned settings; conditioning on them can introduce post-treatment bias.
- Interactions may be numerous, making exhaustive factorial testing impractical.
- Coupon results may not generalize to larger components, complex toolpaths, different thicknesses, multiaxial loads, fatigue, creep, or environmental aging.
- Tensile standards may not fully capture anisotropic or heterogeneous printed structures.
- Even a well-executed mediation analysis may not uniquely identify microscopic mechanisms without direct intervention on the mediator.
- Persistent unexplained variance may reflect latent material heterogeneity, high-frequency process fluctuations, or measurement limits rather than a single omitted cause.

