- Conclusions apply primarily to the selected polymer formulation, geometry, slicer, printer architecture, and process range.
- Some process variables cannot be randomized at the coupon level, so build-level clustering may limit power.
- Instrumenting the print process may alter thermal or airflow conditions.
- Internal imaging and microstructural assays may be destructive, sparse, or imperfect proxies for bond strength.
- Feedstock lots and nominally identical printers may contain unmeasured compositional or hardware differences.
- Actual process variables are often consequences of assigned settings; conditioning on them can introduce post-treatment bias.
- Interactions may be numerous, making exhaustive factorial testing impractical.
- Coupon results may not generalize to larger components, complex toolpaths, different thicknesses, multiaxial loads, fatigue, creep, or environmental aging.
- Tensile standards may not fully capture anisotropic or heterogeneous printed structures.
- Even a well-executed mediation analysis may not uniquely identify microscopic mechanisms without direct intervention on the mediator.
- Persistent unexplained variance may reflect latent material heterogeneity, high-frequency process fluctuations, or measurement limits rather than a single omitted cause.
